% Natalie Curtis Burlin, Songs and Tales from the Dark Continent (1920): Ndau songs sung by C. Kamba Simango and a Zulu song by Madikane Cele.
% Published early from the workshop on 11 November 2026: each tune names what is guessed or left out in its N: lines,
% and its workshop record (or review item) is kept for further correction.

X:1
T:Cha-ko'mba, ko'mba
T:Hopping song: Ndau children's game
C:traditional (Ndau), as sung by C. Kamba Simango
O:Mozambique (then Portuguese East Africa), Ndau people
R:singing game
S:Natalie Curtis Burlin, Songs and Tales from the Dark Continent (New York: G. Schirmer, 1920), Children's Songs II, p. 106 (game notes p. 31)
N:An evening game of Ndau children in central Mozambique: everyone hops on one leg in time to the song, racing to a goal, and whoever puts the other foot down loses (Curtis, p. 31). C. Kamba Simango sang it for Curtis; her 1920 book carries the colonial and racial language of its time in its title and commentary, so teach it as Simango's song, with the game. "Cha-komba-komba" means to hop on one leg; "Cha m'nthalila" means "This is Mnthalila's hop" (a name from the verb "to jump"); the "th" is a t followed by a breathed h, not the English sound. The second line is Curtis's singing English. The soft thump on each beat, for the hops, is ours; the plate has the voice alone. The Chindau spelling wants a Chindau speaker's check.
M:3/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=116
K:Ab
