X:3
T:Mu-to-to-li'le
T:Drip-drop the rain: Ndau children's song
C:traditional (Ndau), as sung by C. Kamba Simango
O:Mozambique (then Portuguese East Africa), Ndau people
R:children's song
S:Natalie Curtis Burlin, Songs and Tales from the Dark Continent (New York: G. Schirmer, 1920), Children's Songs I, p. 106 (game notes p. 30)
N:Ndau children in central Mozambique sing this while playing in the rain, calling the drops to pour down until the pools are full, so they can swim and hunt the big red-breasted bullfrogs (Curtis, p. 30). "To-to" is the sound of rain falling in drops. C. Kamba Simango sang it for Curtis; her 1920 book carries the colonial and racial language of its time in its title and commentary, so teach it as Simango's song, with the game. The accents are Curtis's stress marks over every "To" and "to": the stress falls on the pickup and on the downbeat, against the bar. The plate opens with a bar of rests before the first "To", written here as a pickup. Sung unaccompanied; the second line is Curtis's singing English. The Chindau spelling and stresses want a Chindau speaker's check.
