X:4
T:O tu'la, mntwa'na (Thula, mntwana)
T:O hush thee, baby: Zulu lullaby
C:traditional (Zulu), as sung by Madikane Cele
O:South Africa (Zululand / KwaZulu-Natal)
R:lullaby
S:Natalie Curtis Burlin, Songs and Tales from the Dark Continent (New York: G. Schirmer, 1920), "Igama la Bantwana: Song of Children (Lullaby)", p. 135; words and notes pp. 66-68
N:A Zulu lullaby "very commonly used to put Zulu babies to sleep", sung for Curtis by Madikane Cele: the mother has crossed the mountains and another woman (a grandmother, an aunt) quiets the crying child: your mother is not with you, the zig-zag trail has held her; she is coming soon and will bring you pretty berries. Curtis's 1920 book carries the colonial and racial language of its time in its title and commentary, so teach it as Cele's song. Sing it softly, swaying on the dotted quarters, with no accompaniment. Each "I-wa!" is a long cooing glide down from B to E, slid rather than stepped, with the last two slowing (rit.). The first note of bar 13 ("yo") is between D sharp and E in Curtis's plate, written as D sharp. The Zulu is in Curtis's 1920 spelling (today "Thula, mntwana"); the second line is her singing English, which she says follows the Zulu accents. The spelling and words want a Zulu speaker's check, and this tune may differ from the lullaby "Thula Mntwana" sung today.
M:6/8
L:1/8
Q:3/8=50
